# 12 — Image Layers and Build Cache

## What Are Image Layers?

Docker images are built from filesystem layers and image configuration. Many Dockerfile instructions create filesystem changes that can be stored in layers.

Layers can be reused across images, reducing duplicate storage.

## What Is Build Cache?

Docker can reuse previous build results when the relevant instructions and their inputs have not changed.

## Example Dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM ubuntu:24.04

RUN apt-get update && \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl && \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

WORKDIR /app

COPY app.sh .

CMD ["bash", "app.sh"]
```

## Build the Image

```bash
docker build --progress=plain -t cache-demo:v1 .
docker build --progress=plain -t cache-demo:v1 .
```

The second build may reuse cached steps.

## Inspect Image History

```bash
docker history cache-demo:v1
```

## Best Practices

- Place stable dependency-installation steps before frequently changing source files when practical.
- Combine related package-management commands.
- Remove unnecessary temporary files.
- Avoid invalidating expensive cache steps unnecessarily.
- Remember that cache behavior depends on the instruction and its inputs.

## Practice Tasks

1. Build an image twice.
2. Change only the application file and rebuild.
3. Observe which steps are cached.
4. Compare the image history before and after changing the Dockerfile.
5. Explain why build caching helps CI/CD pipelines.
